'''unacquaintance''' (un-a-kwān′tans), ''n.'' Want of acquaintance or familiarity; lack of knowledge; ignorance. ''Trench'', Study of Words, p. 153.

'''unacquainted''' (un-a̤-kwān′ted), ''a.'' '''1†.''' Not well known; unusual; strange.
{{smaller block|Kiss the lips of ''unacquainted'' change.
{{right|''Shak.'', K. John, iii. 4. 166.}}}}
'''2.''' Not acquainted, or without acquaintance: usually followed by ''with.''
{{smaller block|Bounded on the South-east side with a bay of the Tyrrhen Sea ''unacquainted'' with tempests.
{{right|''Sandys'', Travailes (1652), p. 198.}}
Being a Londoner, though altogether ''unacquainted'', I have requested his company at supper.
{{right|''Dekker and Webster'', Northward Ho, i. 1.}}}}

