¶
'''unacquaintedness''' (un-a̤-kwān′ted-nes), ''n.'' The state of being unacquainted. ''South'', Sermons, xl. 9.

'''unacquirable''' (un-a̤-kwīr′a̤-bl), ''a.'' Not acquirable.

'''unacquirableness''' (un-a̤-kwīr′a̤-bl-nes), ''n.'' The character of being unacquirable. ''A. Tucker'', Light of Nature, xviii.

'''unacquired''' (un-a̤-kwīrd′), ''a.'' Not acquired; not gained. ''Jer. Taylor.''

'''unacted''' (un-ak′ted), ''a.'' Not acted; not performed; not executed.
{{smaller block|The fault unknown is as a thought ''unacted.''
{{right|''Shak.'', Lucrece, l. 527.}}}}
{{smaller block|[Often used with ''on'' or ''upon'', then signifying not affected (by): as, a metal ''unacted upon'' by an acid.}}
{{smaller block|An extremely good non-conductor of electricity is ''unacted upon'' by acids or alkalies, and is therefore adapted for making galvanic batteries.
{{right|''Jour. Franklin Inst.'', CXXVI. 261.]}}}}

'''unactive†''' (un-ak′tiv), ''a.'' Not active; inactive. {{smaller|(''a'') Listless; not active or acting; slothful.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Think you me so tame,
So leaden and ''unactive'', to sit down
With such dishonour?</poem>
{{right|''Fletcher (and another?)'', Prophetess, v. 1.}}}}
{{smaller|(''b'') Inoperative; not producing effects; having no efficacy.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>In the fruitful earth . . .
His beams, ''unactive'' else, their vigour find.</poem>
{{right|''Milton'', P. L., viii. 97.}}}}
{{smaller|(''c'') Marked by inaction; not utilized.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>While useless words consume th’ ''unactive'' hours,
No wonder Troy so long resists our pow’rs.</poem>
{{right|''Pope'', Iliad, ii. 408.}}}}
